module gunbc.host_disk_reclaim

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size, byte_size_count, Second, second, seconds_per_day }
import gunbc.build_cache_instance { RunnerSlotIdentity }

// THE FLEET'S DISK CAPACITY MECHANISM, SUBSUMED. This module models what
// ctrl-runner-reclaim.timer has been doing on every runner host since 2026-07-23, read from the
// script itself (scripts/session-dashboard/host/reclaim-runner-disk.sh and its .service/.timer)
// rather than from a description of it. It follows the precedent gunbc.runner_lifecycle
// runner_lifecycle_ctrl_void_note sets for exactly this situation: READ the ctrl artifact as ground
// truth for what the host actually does, subsume it here, and never invoke ctrl from gunbc.
//
// WHY THIS IS A FABRIC CONCERN AND NOT HOUSEKEEPING. The runners are ephemeral in IDENTITY -- a
// one-shot JIT registration per job -- but their WORKSPACE is deliberately persistent: each slot
// keeps a fixed _work checkout so CI does not re-clone the ~10G repo and rebuild cold every job.
// That trade was taken on purpose and nothing bounded its cost side, so a slot's own growth is a
// standing claim on the host's disk. A fabric that allocates slots without modeling that claim is
// allocating against a number that drifts underneath it.
//
// AND IT IS ALREADY LOAD-BEARING, WHICH IS THE REASON TO MODEL IT RATHER THAN REPLACE IT. Measured
// on srv4 (operator, 2026-08-22): packs are 15-46 per checkout against the ~370 the script's own
// header describes, and slots are 2.5G against the 13G it describes. The slots are small BECAUSE
// this runs. Deleting it re-opens a 5x disk regression on every host, so this model exists to give
// the mechanism a home in the substrate, not to supersede a working thing with an unproven one.
//
// NOT A SECOND HYGIENE AUTHORITY. gunbc.host_hygiene_reaper is a DIFFERENT mechanism that shares
// the word: it reaps residual SLOTS -- stale cgroups, inactive units, control-override dirs -- and
// carries zero coverage of packs, repack, tmp_pack, _diag or docker (verified by grep against both
// it and its _remediate half, all six terms zero). Treating one as the other's successor is the
// one-name-two-concepts error, so this module states the boundary rather than assuming a reader
// infers it.
data host_disk_reclaim_subsumption_note: String = "Subsumes ctrl-runner-reclaim.timer / .service / reclaim-runner-disk.sh, read as ground truth per gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_lifecycle_ctrl_void_note. The script is the authority for WHAT the host does today; this module is the authority for what the fabric believes about it. Disjoint from gunbc.host_hygiene_reaper, which reaps residual slots and models no disk reclamation."

// THE FIVE GROWTH PATHS, each a separate arm because each has a different owner and a different
// repair. Four are the accretion the script's header names; the fifth is a WEDGE rather than
// growth, and it is the one a summary of this mechanism is most likely to drop: a review killed
// mid-run leaks a refs/heads/review/* ref, and once its target object goes missing `git gc` dies
// with a bad-object error AND writes a .git/gc.log that DISABLES ALL FUTURE AUTO-GC. So the failure
// is self-perpetuating: the mechanism that would reclaim the disk is exactly what the wedge turns
// off, and every subsequent review on that runner stays wedged until a human intervenes.
type DiskReclaimConcern
  = UnrepackedPackAccretion { pack_count: Int }
  | InterruptedFetchResidue { tmp_pack_count: Int }
  | UnrotatedDiagnosticLogs { log_bytes: ByteSize }
  | DockerBuildCacheAccretion { rootless: Bool }
  | WedgedObjectStore { cause: StoreWedgeCause }

type StoreWedgeCause
  = BrokenReviewRef { ref_name: NonEmptyStr }
  | PoisonedGcLog

// A SLOT RUNNING A JOB IS NOT A SLOT WHOSE STATE IS UNKNOWN, and collapsing the two is what would
// make this mechanism unsafe. The script decides busy-ness by cgroup -- every Runner.Worker lives
// in its slot's actions-runner@<slot>.service cgroup -- which is an observation that can itself
// fail. Busy means "do not touch git here"; unobservable means "we could not tell", and both refuse
// git work while being different facts with different repairs (DESIGN section 5: a failure arm
// refuses, it never widens, and could-not-observe must never render as a verdict about the subject).
// WHY THIS IS NOT std.upsert_decision.ObservationVerdict, asked by review 54879 and worth answering
// at the declaration because the two genuinely are adjacent. ObservationVerdict answers "does this
// subject AGREE WITH ITS DESIRED STATE" -- Converged, Drifted, Conflict, Inaccessible, UnknownRefused
// -- and it is the right authority wherever a fabric cell is being reconciled. SlotJobState answers
// something else entirely: what is this slot DOING RIGHT NOW, as an INPUT to a decision about whether
// we may touch it. A busy slot is not drifted and not unconverged; it is working exactly as intended,
// and reporting it as a convergence verdict would make correct operation read as a fault. The overlap
// is real but partial -- the unobservable arm alone rhymes with Inaccessible -- and grounding a
// whole three-arm operational state on a six-arm convergence vocabulary to capture one arm would be
// the hollow alias, not single authority. The import that prompted the question was simply dead and
// is deleted.
type SlotJobState
  = SlotRunningJob { worker_cgroup_unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | SlotIdle
  | SlotJobStateUnobservable { reason: NonEmptyStr }

type CheckoutObservation {
  git_dir: NonEmptyStr
  owner: NonEmptyStr
  pack_count: Int
  tmp_pack_count: Int
  git_dir_bytes: ByteSize
}

type SlotDiskObservation {
  slot: RunnerSlotIdentity
  job_state: SlotJobState
  diag_log_bytes: ByteSize
  checkouts: List<CheckoutObservation>
}

// THE PARAMETERS ARE THE SCRIPT'S DEFAULTS, CARRIED AS DATA RATHER THAN RESTATED IN PROSE, so a
// consumer reads the same number the host runs on. Each is an operator override in the real unit
// (EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/ctrl-runner-reclaim), which is why they are declarations here and
// not literals inlined at their use sites.
data reclaim_fragmentation_gate_packs: Int = 8

data reclaim_diag_max_age: Second = second(count: 3 * seconds_per_day())

data reclaim_tmp_pack_min_age: Second = second(count: 1 * seconds_per_day())

data reclaim_per_repo_timeout: Second = second(count: 1800)

// THE TWO AGE FLOORS ARE DURATIONS AND NOW CARRY ONE (review 54879). They first landed as
// `_days: Int`, a flat scalar with the unit in the identifier -- which is the unit-modeling trigger,
// and it sat directly beside `reclaim_per_repo_timeout: Second` doing the same job correctly, so
// the file disagreed with itself about whether a duration has a carrier. The day grain the script
// authors in is preserved by CONSTRUCTION rather than by a hand-computed literal: 3 * seconds_per_day()
// is legible as "three days" and cannot drift from the second count the way a transcribed 259200
// could. seconds_per_day is composed in std.measure from the ISO 8601 authority that already grounds
// seconds-per-minute and minutes-per-hour there; hours-per-day was missing from that citation and is
// added to it, so no new constant is minted here or anywhere.
data reclaim_parameters_note: String = "RECLAIM_GIT_GC_MIN_PACKS=8, RECLAIM_DIAG_MAX_AGE_DAYS=3, RECLAIM_GIT_GC_TIMEOUT_S=1800, and the tmp_pack sweep's -mtime +1. The pack gate is a FRAGMENTATION gate, not a size gate: a repo at or below it is skipped because the I/O is not worth it, which is what makes a re-run cheap and idempotent. The tmp_pack age floor exists so the sweep can never race a live index-pack."

// THE DECISION IS FOUR-WAY AND THE TWO REFUSALS ARE NOT ONE ARM. Warranted and below-gate are both
// answers about the repo; busy and unobservable are answers about whether we may ask at all. An
// earlier shape that returned a Bool could not say which, and a consumer reading false could not
// tell "already packed" from "we did not look".
type RepackDecision
  = RepackWarranted { git_dir: NonEmptyStr, owner: NonEmptyStr, pack_count: Int }
  | RepackBelowFragmentationGate { git_dir: NonEmptyStr, pack_count: Int }
  | RepackRefusedSlotBusy { git_dir: NonEmptyStr, worker_cgroup_unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | RepackRefusedJobStateUnobservable { git_dir: NonEmptyStr, reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn repack_decision_for(job_state: SlotJobState, checkout: CheckoutObservation) -> RepackDecision {
  match job_state {
    SlotRunningJob { worker_cgroup_unit: unit } =>
      RepackRefusedSlotBusy { git_dir: checkout.git_dir, worker_cgroup_unit: unit }
    SlotJobStateUnobservable { reason: r } =>
      RepackRefusedJobStateUnobservable { git_dir: checkout.git_dir, reason: r }
    SlotIdle =>
      if checkout.pack_count > reclaim_fragmentation_gate_packs {
        RepackWarranted {
          git_dir: checkout.git_dir,
          owner: checkout.owner,
          pack_count: checkout.pack_count,
        }
      } else {
        RepackBelowFragmentationGate { git_dir: checkout.git_dir, pack_count: checkout.pack_count }
      }
  }
}

fn slot_repack_decisions(obs: SlotDiskObservation) -> List<RepackDecision> {
  map(obs.checkouts, c => repack_decision_for(job_state: obs.job_state, checkout: c))
}

// THE TMP_PACK SWEEP AND THE DIAG ROTATION RUN REGARDLESS OF BUSY-NESS, and that is a property of
// the subjects rather than an exception to the safety rule: neither is job state. A leftover
// tmp_pack_* is pure garbage from an interrupted index-pack, and _diag holds logs, so removing
// either cannot disturb a running job. Only the git object store is serialised against live work.
fn slot_has_interrupted_fetch_residue(obs: SlotDiskObservation) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: obs.checkouts,
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(acc, c) { acc || c.tmp_pack_count > 0 },
  )
}

data reclaim_unconditional_arms_note: String = "tmp_pack sweep and _diag rotation are not gated on slot busy-ness because their subjects are not job state -- an interrupted index-pack's leftover and a log file are safe to remove under a running job. The git object store is the only subject serialised against live work, and it is serialised twice over: skipped entirely for a busy slot, and left to git's own repo lock otherwise."

// WHAT THE FABRIC ACTUALLY WANTS FROM THIS: a per-slot disk claim derived from OBSERVATION rather
// than from arithmetic. gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_slot_disk_budget_per_slot is today a single
// literal, byte_size(40960000000), whose own annotation derives it as "~38GB per slot at width 7 on
// 500GB class disk" -- that is the disk divided by the width you want, then used to preflight
// whether that width fits, so the charge and the question are the same number. Measured reality on
// srv4 is ~2.5G per slot, about sixteen times smaller, and the literal post-dates this reclaimer by
// two weeks -- so it was never a stale measurement, it was never a measurement at all.
//
// This fold is the replacement's input, not the replacement: it says what a slot currently occupies,
// which is the quantity a width preflight should charge. Retiring the literal is deliberately NOT
// done here, because the operator's sequencing is that the reclaimer is subsumed before anything
// touches the width numbers -- those numbers are downstream of whether this mechanism runs.
fn slot_observed_bytes(obs: SlotDiskObservation) -> ByteSize {
  byte_size(
    count: fold_list(
      xs: obs.checkouts,
      empty: byte_size_count(b: obs.diag_log_bytes),
      cons: fn(acc, c) { acc + byte_size_count(b: c.git_dir_bytes) },
    ),
  )
}

data reclaim_budget_supersession_note: String = "THE INTENDED CONSUMER, named so this fold is not mistaken for a report. gunbc.runner_lifecycle runner_slot_disk_budget_per_slot is circular -- disk divided by desired width, used to preflight that width -- and measures ~16x the observed per-slot occupancy. slot_observed_bytes is the observed quantity that replaces it. NOT DONE IN THIS MODULE, by operator sequencing 2026-08-22: the reclaimer is subsumed first, because the width literals are downstream of whether reclamation runs at all. A width charge derived from observation while the mechanism holding that observation down is unmodeled would be a number resting on an unmodeled dependency."
